- other core features:
    - function pointers
    - generics
    - context system
    - errors

- non-core features:
    - uniform function call syntax
    - chained template inference. the compiler will infer the template type from the context. example:
            var x: i32 = "5".parse()
        typically you'd have to call it such as parse<i32>() but this will infer it from the context.
        the reason why it is called chained template inference is because this can be combined with
        uniform function call syntax to keep updating the context type so a template rarely has to be called such as:
            var x: i32 = "5".parse.mul.log10
        the biggest benefit of this is it makes long declarative code very readable and simple because
        the type is only specified once.
    - defer
    - union literals A | B
    - unions
    - error unions
    - pattern matching
    - comptime blocks
    - closures
    - tuples and destructuring
    - string interpolation
    - module initializers. and per thread module initializers

